<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Statuta Valachorum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1630">1630</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Vlach Statute</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Statuta Valachorum, or Vlach Statute, was a charter issued by Emperor Ferdinand II in 1630 for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Vlach and Serb settlers of the Croatian and Slavonian Military Frontier. It confirmed their privileges, including freedom from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>, their own elected judges and captains and the right to keep their land in return for military service. It placed them directly under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> military authorities rather than the Croatian nobility.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Military Frontier was set up in the mid-16th century as a buffer against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, and Balkan refugees, mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlachs">Vlachs</a>, settled there; the sources call the fugitives Vlachs almost without exception. Military colonists were exempted from some obligations, granted small tracts of land and allowed to elect their own captains (vojvode) and magistrates (knezovi). A large migration of Serbs into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a> took place in 1600, and freedom of religion was promised to all Orthodox settlers.</p><p>The Croatian nobility, stripped of its authority in the Frontier, sought its incorporation into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. A law of Rudolf II in 1608, under which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlachs">Vlachs</a> owed a tenth of their income to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> and a ninth to the landlords, had little practical effect. In 1627 the Varaždin frontiersmen declared they would rather be hacked into pieces than become subjects of the Croatian nobility, and Ferdinand II granted the Vlachs the right to stay undisturbed in their settlements.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The statute was signed in Regensburg on 5 October 1630 and applied to the Varaždin Generalate, the captaincies of Koprivnica, Križevci and Ivanec. It was a compromise drawn from the frontiersmen's own draft and petitions and was handed to a delegation of twelve frontiersmen, military commanders and clergy. It allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlachs">Vlachs</a> to elect their local authorities, knezes and judges, and gave them a civil administration based on their traditional law, in return for military service owed by all males over sixteen. It also drew the first boundary of the Varaždin Generalate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The statute's goal was to bring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlachs">Vlachs</a> under the supervision of the imperial court while giving them an appearance of autonomy, though the self-government they had held before actually decreased. A rebellion in the generalate in 1632 was suppressed, and the knez Marko Bogdanović and the harambaša Smiljan Vujica were executed. Another rising followed in 1665–66 under Stefan Osmokruhović, and the statute was revised on 14 April 1667.</p><p>First applied only to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlachs">Vlachs</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drava">Drava</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a>, it later came to be used by all Vlachs. It is seen as the first public law document on the rights of citizens within the Military Frontier, and the warrior tradition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, which includes the statute, remains an important part of the community's identity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Statuta Valachorum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
